The behavior of mass ejection described here is in qualitative agreement with the previous full-GR (Hotokezaka et al., 2013b, for soft EOSs) and approximate GR (Bauswein et al., 2013, for the same SFHo EOS) works. As pointed out in these studies, the mass ejection is due to shock-heating and tidal torque; neutrino-heating plays a subdominant role.

As the HMNS forms, temperature near its surface gets as high as \\sim 10 MeV (\\sim 100 GK), giving rise to copious e^{-}e^{+} pairs that activate the weak interactions n+e^{+}\\rightarrow\\bar{\\nu}_{e}+p, p+e^{-}\\rightarrow\\nu_{e}+n, and their inverses. The e^{+} and \\nu_{e} captures convert some part of neutrons to protons; the ejecta Y_{\\rm e}'s increase from the initial low values.2 The first outgoing ejecta from the contact interface region are away from the HMNS when it forms and thus neutrino capture is subdominant. As a result, the Y_{\\rm e}'s are relatively low (\\sim 0.1-0.2; blue-cyan spiral arms in Figure 2). The outer ejecta with higher Y_{\\rm e} (\\sim 0.2-0.3) are unimportant in the total ejecta mass because of their low densities.

Footnote 2: The fast moving NSs and subsequent merger ejecta in the background medium make shocks that can increase temperature and thus Y_{\\rm e}. However, the mass suffering from these artifacts is negligibly small compared to the total ejecta mass (Figure 1).

In the second phase of mass ejection, neutrinos coming from the HMNS surface play a crucial role. The luminosities and mean energies are only slightly greater for \\bar{\\nu}_{e} than those for \\nu_{e} (Figure 1). The asymptotic

