The ejecta mass distributions in Y_{\\rm e} and S/k_{\\rm B} at the end of simulation are displayed in Figure 3 for the x-y, x-z, and y-z planes. We find that the Y_{\\rm e}'s widely distribute between 0.09 and 0.45 with greater amounts for higher Y_{\\rm e}, in which the initial \\beta-equilibrium values (\\approx 0.06) have gone. Non-orbital ejecta have higher Y_{\\rm e}'s because of the shock-heated matter escaping to the low-density polar regions (Hotokezaka et al., 2013b). The shock heating results in S/k_{\\rm B} up to \\approx 26 and 50 for the orbital and non-orbital planes, respectively (with higher values for higher Y_{\\rm e}), which are sizably greater than those in Goriely et al. (2011, S/k_{\\rm B}\\sim 1-3) with the Shen's EOS.

## 3. The r-process

The nucleosynthesis analysis makes use of the thermodynamic trajectories of the ejecta particles traced on the orbital plane. A representative particle is chosen from each Y_{\\rm e}-bin (from Y_{\\rm e}=0.09 to 0.44 with the interval of \\Delta Y_{\\rm e}=0.01 (Figure 3). For simplicity, we analyze only the x-y components because of the dominance of the ejecta masses close to the orbital plane. Each nucleosynthesis calculation is initiated when the temperature decreases to 10 GK, where the initial composition is given by Y_{\\rm e} and 1-Y_{\\rm e} for the mass fractions of free protons and neutrons.

The reaction network consists of 6300 species from single neutrons and protons to the Z=110 isotopes. Experimental rates, when available, are taken from the latest versions of REACLIB2(Cyburt et al., 2010) and Nuclear Wallet Cards 8. Otherwise, the theoretical estimates of fusion rates9(TALYS, Goriely et al., 2008) and \\beta-decay half-lives (GT2, Tachibana et al., 1990) are adopted, where both are based on the same nuclear masses (HFB-21, Goriely et al., 2010). Theoretical fission properties adopted are those estimated on the basis of the HFB-14 mass model. For fission fragments, a Gaussian-type distribution is assumed with emission of four prompt neutrons per event. Neutrino captures are not included, which make only slight shifts of Y_{\\rm e} (typically an increase of \\sim 0.01 from 10 GK to 5 GK).

